<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Serbia</h1><p class="st-meta">2006 CE – present · capital Belgrade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Present-day Serbia, independent successor of the Yugoslav states since 2006, an EU candidate that does not recognise Kosovo's independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> voted to leave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia-and-montenegro">Serbia and Montenegro</a> in 2006, Serbia became the legal successor of the union and of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslav states</a> on 5 June 2006. A new constitution adopted in October 2006 described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, then under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> administration, as an integral part of Serbia.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Serbia is a parliamentary republic with a directly elected president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> declared independence in 2008, which Serbia rejects, though the EU-brokered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels-agreement-2013">Brussels Agreement</a> of 2013 began a normalisation of relations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandar-vucic">Aleksandar Vučić's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbian-progressive-party">Serbian Progressive Party</a> has dominated politics since 2012, combining EU accession talks with close ties to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Manufacturing, agriculture and IT drive the economy.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Serbia became an EU candidate in 2012 and opened accession negotiations in 2014. The collapse of a railway station canopy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a> in November 2024, which killed sixteen people, led to the largest student-led protests in decades against corruption and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandar-vucic">Vučić</a>'s government.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/serbia.jpg" alt="Serbia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2006 CE – present</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belgrade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Boris Tadić, Tomislav Nikolić, Aleksandar Vučić</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Serbia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2016&amp;focus=serbia" aria-label="Serbia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 594 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Serbia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5488 850L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5488 850h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Belgrade. <a href="/atlas?year=2016&amp;focus=serbia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
